ActualOutput_Q4:
The annual monitoring report on the implementation of the stakeholder plan was developed as targeted. 

Notable engagements held during the period under review include:

•	Fifth Virtual meeting of the COMESA-EAC-SADC Tripartite Council of Ministers;
•	Presidential meeting for Large Exporters on Rail and Ports;
•	Special SANSS meeting;
•	Presidential Infrastructure Championing Initiative in the North-South Corridors Road, Rail and related infrastructure;
•	Meeting with Gauteng Province, National Treasury, Provincial Gauteng Treasury and SANRAL on e-tolls;
•	Africa Rail Conference;
•	Transport Roundtable and Summit Riverside Irene;
•	International Civil Aviation Day; and
•	Launch of the Taxi Relief Fund.

The progress report on the implementation of the Revised DoT gender-responsive Strategic Plan (2020-2025) was developed as targeted.

The report indicates the following:

•	During the period under review, a 14.7% reduction in road fatalities was recorded. This was a 5.5% reduction from the 20,27% recorded in 2020/21;

•	9 778 205 driving licence cards were produced in the period under review, which was an increase of 7 994 391 from the reported 1 783 814 March 2019 baseline;

•	The turnaround time for issuance of driving license cards was recorded at 11 days for the period under review, which was a 31 day decrease from the March 2019 baseline of 42 days;

•	As at December 2023, 53 580 jobs were created through the SANRAL Road Maintenance Programme, which was a 41 580 increase from the 12 000 March 2019 baseline;

•	As at December 2023, the total number of jobs created through the Provincial Road Maintenance Programme since 2019 was 654 586;

•	The Private Sector Participation (PSP) Framework was approved by Cabinet;

•	A total of 115 275 (direct and indirect jobs) were supported through the ACSA Infrastructure Programme, which was an increase of 84 591 from the March baseline of 30 684; 

•	A pilot phase roll out of the Integrated Single Ticketing System was monitored in Rustenburg and Polokwane municipalities; and

•	The Taxi Recapitalisation South Africa (TRSA) has been established in line with the Taxi Lekgotla Resolutions.

A total of fifteen (15) vacant positions were filled during the period under review. These included:

•	Seven (07) new appointments;
•	Three (03) internal promotions;
•	Two (02) contract appointments; and 
•	Three (03) transfers into the Department

As at 31 March 2024, the vacancy rate of the Department was 15.01% on funded posts.

There were no new cases of irregular expenditure during the period under review.

The report indicates the following:

•	As at 31 March 2024, the Department had nineteen (19) cases of irregular expenditure;

•	Two (02) cases related to non-compliance with local content regulations. A request has been routed to the Accounting Officer requesting removal;

•	Eight (08) cases were referred to Labour Relations of which three (03) are still in progress, and five (05) are being referred to the Department of Higher Education and Training to institute disciplinary processes. A request for intervention to the Accounting Officer has been routed in this regard.

